{"candidates":[{"claim":"As n grows, the probability of reaching unanimous consensus tends to 0.","mechanism":"The number of bichromatic edges is a Lyapunov function: each update of a vertex to the majority of its three neighbors strictly decreases this energy. The process therefore stops at a locally stable mixed configuration. On a random 3-regular graph there are exponentially many such stable mixed obstructions, so the descent is trapped with probability 1-o(1) and consensus probability vanishes.","counterconditions":["If the graph were bipartite or had no cycles, stable mixed obstructions would be absent and consensus could occur.","If the initial coloring were sufficiently biased, one color could take over before trapping."],"typicality":0.9,"optional_refs":["SRC_003"]},{"claim":"The finite obstruction structures are exactly the locally stable mixed configurations in which every vertex has at most one neighbor of the opposite color; equivalently, the bichromatic edges form a matching and each monochromatic induced subgraph has minimum degree at least 2. The minimal such structures are prisms: two monochromatic cycles connected by a perfect matching.","mechanism":"A vertex keeps its color under majority update iff at least two of its three neighbors share its color, so stability forces at most one cross-edge per vertex. In a 3-regular graph this means cross-edges are a matching; removing them leaves components of minimum degree 2, whose finite cores are cycles.","counterconditions":["If a vertex has two or more opposite neighbors it is unstable and will flip.","If the cross-matching is not perfect, some monochromatic components contain degree-3 vertices."],"typicality":0.85},{"claim":"The expected number of locally stable mixed configurations scales as exp(Θ(n)); their fraction among all 2^n colorings is exp(-Θ(n)).","mechanism":"For a fixed coloring, stability is the event that bichromatic edges form a matching. The number of matchings in a random 3-regular graph is exp(c n) with c≈0.58, so the expected number of stable colorings is 2^n * 2^{-3n/2} * exp(c n) = exp((ln2 - 1.5 ln2 + c)n) = exp(Θ(n)).","counterconditions":["If the graph were a fixed bounded-degree expander with few matchings, the rate could change.","The law is for random 3-regular graphs; deterministic graphs may have different rates."],"typicality":0.8,"optional_refs":["SRC_004"]},{"claim":"As n grows, the probability of reaching unanimous consensus tends to 1.","mechanism":"The process is a random descent on energy; the global minimum (unanimous) has zero energy and the stable mixed states are rare enough that the process escapes or avoids them, so consensus occurs whp.","counterconditions":["This would fail if stable mixed states have large basins.","Numerical simulations for small n may show trapping."],"typicality":0.1},{"claim":"The probability of reaching unanimous consensus tends to a constant strictly between 0 and 1.","mechanism":"The process has a positive probability to reach consensus before hitting a stable mixed obstruction, and a positive probability to get trapped; the limit is the probability that the initial descent avoids all traps.","counterconditions":["If the number of traps grows exponentially, the avoidance probability may vanish.","If traps are negligible, it may tend to 1."],"typicality":0.15},{"claim":"The probability of consensus is exp(-Θ(n)), and the final number of bichromatic edges in a trapped state is Θ(n) with high probability.","mechanism":"The energy descent stops at a matching of size Θ(n); the probability of reaching zero energy requires eliminating all bichromatic edges, which is exponentially unlikely because the process must pass through a bottleneck where one color has taken over almost all vertices.","counterconditions":["If the initial coloring is not uniform but has a large majority, consensus probability may be 1-o(1).","If the graph has no stable mixed obstructions, consensus occurs."],"typicality":0.3}]}